      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Since Jordan is fixed to run the fourth lap, we only need to consider the order of the other three runners (Team Member 1, Team Member 2, and Team Member 3).\n\nTo find the number of different orders for the other three runners, we can use the concept of permutations. Specifically, we need to find the number of ways to arrange three runners in a sequence.\n\nThe number of permutations of \\( n \\) objects is given by \\( n! \\) (n factorial), which is the product of all positive integers up to \\( n \\). In this case, \\( n = 3 \\), so we need to calculate \\( 3! \\):\n\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, there are 6 different orders in which Jordan and his three best friends can run, with Jordan running the fourth lap.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
