    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the number of different orders in which Jordan's team members can run the race, given that Jordan is the only one running the fourth lap, we need to calculate the number of permutations of the three teammates. The position of Jordan, who must run the fourth lap, does not affect the number of possible orders for the other three team members.\n\nThe number of permutations of 3 distinct objects is given by \\(3!\\) (3 factorial), which is calculated as follows:\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nHere is the reasoning step-by-step:\n\n1. **Identify the positions and restrictions**: Jordan's position is fixed as the fourth lap. This leaves the first three positions to be filled by the other three team members.\n\n2. **Determine the number of permutations for the remaining three positions**: The number of ways to arrange 3 distinct objects in 3 positions is given by the factorial of 3, which is \\(3!\\).\n\n3. **Calculate \\(3!\\)**:\n   \\[\n   3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n   \\]\n\nThus, the number of different orders in which Jordan's team members can run, given that Jordan is always running the fourth lap, is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
