    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the number of different orders in which Jordan and his three best friends can run the relay race, we need to account for the fact that Jordan will run the fourth lap. This means we only need to arrange the other three team members (let's call them A, B, and C) in the first three laps. The order in which they run those three laps matters because the positions are distinct.\n\nThe number of ways to arrange 3 distinct individuals in a specific order is given by the factorial of 3, denoted as \\(3!\\). The factorial of a number \\(n\\) is the product of all positive integers up to \\(n\\). Therefore, we calculate \\(3!\\) as follows:\n\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nSo, there are 6 different ways to arrange the first three runners (A, B, and C) in the first three laps. Since Jordan is fixed in the fourth position, the total number of different orders in which the four team members can run the relay race is simply the number of ways to arrange the first three runners, which is 6.\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
